    {
      "filename": "Skoteini-Alea-Polje Peloponnese Greece.jpg",
      "title": "File:Skoteini-Alea-Polje Peloponnese Greece.jpg",
      "url": "https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/c/ca/Skoteini-Alea-Polje_Peloponnese_Greece.jpg",
      "descriptionUrl": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Skoteini-Alea-Polje_Peloponnese_Greece.jpg",
      "width": 1800,
      "height": 1200,
      "mime": "image/jpeg",
      "license": "CC BY-SA 3.0 de",
      "licenseUrl": "https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/3.0/de/deed.en",
      "artist": "ulrichstill",
      "description": "View on part of the plateau  Skotiní-Aléa, Argolis (a valleylike 14 km long karst depression, 560 m, polje), on all sides surrounded by mountains. There are two extensions off the valley, where the two villages Skotiní and Aléa rest on steep slopes. Relatively frequent geological phenomenon in the karst basins of the northeast Peloponnese, Greece.\nThe valley plateau has no natural surface water outlet. Precipitation must be drained by ditches. When precipitation of winters is exceptionally rich, some agricultural fields may become wet. Depending on the capacity of the ditches and the two geologically old ponors, some fields may even be flooded. The southern section of the plateau has an up to 30 m extra deep and 2 km long depression, in which precipitation creates a temporary lake, for which the ponor must turn active. See the Foto: Image:Alea Argolidas.JPG.",
      "dateCreated": "2007-04-25",
      "sourceType": "commons-category",
      "sourceArticleId": "argolis-wk",
      "sourceGeohash4": "sw8n",
      "smallPath": "_m/s/w/8/n/argolis-wk/skoteini-alea-polje-peloponnes-small.webp"
    },
    {
      "filename": "Karst-spring-Kefalari Argolis Greece.jpg",
      "title": "File:Karst-spring-Kefalari Argolis Greece.jpg",
      "url": "https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/f/fd/Karst-spring-Kefalari_Argolis_Greece.jpg",
      "descriptionUrl": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Karst-spring-Kefalari_Argolis_Greece.jpg",
      "width": 1800,
      "height": 1200,
      "mime": "image/jpeg",
      "license": "CC BY-SA 3.0 de",
      "licenseUrl": "https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/3.0/de/deed.en",
      "artist": "ulrichstill",
      "description": "karst spring Kefalari (Argolis, 10 km to the coast and the town Argos). The spring discharge is big, but varies seasonally. According to hydrogeological studies (&lt;1984) the subsurface waterways have two origins: Depression Lake Stymphalia and valley like depression of the regional unit Skoteini-Alea (see Alea, Argolis). The subsurface waterways are fed by old katavothren (καταβόθρα, Greek term for ponor), which drain the depressions, when flooded. This was confirmed by dye tracing. Also see the photo: Image:Alea Argolidas.JPG.\nEven in ancient time of Greece  this spring was holy. People in those days suspected, that the water comes from Lake Stymphalia, Spring, cave (60 m, geologically a former waterway), the modern orthodox pilgrimage “Zoodochos Pigi” and the church “Panagia Kefalariotissa” form todays sight.",
      "dateCreated": "2006-05-13",
      "sourceType": "commons-category",
      "sourceArticleId": "kefalari-argolis-wp",
      "sourceGeohash4": "sw8n"
    },
    {
      "filename": "Submarine Karst-spring-Kiveri Peloponnese Greece.jpg",
      "title": "File:Submarine Karst-spring-Kiveri Peloponnese Greece.jpg",
      "url": "https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/c/ca/Submarine_Karst-spring-Kiveri_Peloponnese_Greece.jpg",
      "descriptionUrl": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Submarine_Karst-spring-Kiveri_Peloponnese_Greece.jpg",
      "width": 1800,
      "height": 1200,
      "mime": "image/jpeg",
      "license": "CC BY-SA 3.0 de",
      "licenseUrl": "https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/3.0/de/deed.en",
      "artist": "ulrichstill",
      "description": "Big submarine karst spring next to Kiveri, Argolic Gulf. The precipitation water comes from the karstified limestone mountains of the Northeastern Peloponnese. Gated weirs keep the fresh water table higher to prevent saline sea water from mixing with the fresh water. The water is good for irrigation as it contains only 0.3 to 2.5 gr/l Chloride.",
      "dateCreated": "2006-05-13",
